"learn.topic.matchiq.timeline.3.body" = "The cleanest finish usually comes after a patient shape reset.";
"learn.topic.service_design.title" = "Service Design";
"learn.topic.service_design.summary" = "Explore how serve height, disguise, and first-contact pressure frame the entire point before the crowd notices it.";
"learn.topic.service_design.readingTime" = "6 min read";
"learn.topic.service_design.p1" = "The service in Takraw is not a neutral restart. It is an authored opening move that introduces tempo, trajectory, and emotional pressure in a single action. The best Tekongs are not only accurate; they are persuasive. They make receivers commit early.";
"learn.topic.service_design.p2" = "Serve design combines body mechanics with tactical storytelling. Small variations in toss rhythm, hip position, and contact height can force the receiving side to alter their shape a half-step earlier than they would prefer. In a sport this compressed, half a step is meaningful.";
"learn.topic.service_design.p3" = "A useful way to study service is to ask what kind of second touch it creates. An intimidating serve that produces a comfortable first receive is less valuable than a disciplined serve that bends the next contact toward the sideline and disrupts the Feeder's posture.";
"learn.topic.service_design.p4" = "For the product, this topic gives users a practical bridge between fundamentals and intelligence. It shows that even the earliest touch can be discussed like design, not just technique.";
"learn.topic.service_design.b1" = "A serve should be judged by the shape it forces next, not only by how dramatic it looks.";
"learn.topic.service_design.b2" = "Disguise and timing matter because receivers react before the ball finishes traveling.";
"learn.topic.service_design.b3" = "Service pressure is the first tactical lever available in every rally.";
